::: {.cell}

```{.python .cell-code}
t = np.arange(1, 25)
plt.figure(figsize=(5, 5))
plt.plot(t, Vendite, color="black")
plt.plot(t, mm3, "o--", color="black", markerfacecolor="none", markersize=4)
plt.plot(t, mm5, "-.", color="black", linewidth=2)
plt.title("G.M. sales")
plt.show()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Figure 4.2 – 3- and 5-term moving averages of G.M. sales](04-time-series_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-6-1.png)
:::
:::


### SAS

```sas
proc sgplot data=auto_mm;
  title "G.M. sales";
  series x=Anno y=Vendite;
  series x=Anno y=mm3 / markers lineattrs=(pattern=dash);
  series x=Anno y=mm5 / lineattrs=(pattern=dashdotdot thickness=2);
run;
```
:::

Clearly the 5-term moving average loses more observations but smooths the original data better in search of the underlying tendency. Even stronger smoothing is given by a higher-order average, for example with 9 terms (Figure 4.3).

::: {.panel-tabset group="language"}
