## 4.5 Non-linear trends {#sec-nonlinear-trends}

Since we want to identify the long-run direction of movement without worrying too much about the fit to the data, the trend of a series can generally be represented by a straight line; over the medium to long run, however, it is reasonable to look for non-linear trends too. Looking for a non-linear trend becomes necessary when trend and cycle are not treated as separate components, so that the (non-seasonal) fluctuation of the series must be captured by the estimated model. There is also a strong motivation when looking for a long-run trend: for example, if over 30 years the trend had changed direction halfway through, a linear model might fail to capture the current direction and to forecast the future direction of the series correctly; in such conditions, a non-linear trend would help draw more reliable conclusions.

We load data on Kodak's revenues for 1975-1998, contained in the file *entrate.csv*. We drop the assumption of a linear trend and now also look for a quadratic one. Figure 4.6 shows the original series and the fitted values of the linear trend $Y=a+bt$ and of the quadratic trend $Y=a+bt+ct^{2}$, which immediately appears much more informative and useful for forecasting.

::: {.panel-tabset group="language"}
### R


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
entrate <- read.csv2("data/entrate.csv")
attach(entrate)
trendlin <- lm(Entrate~Codice)
Codice2 <- Codice^2
trendqua <- lm(Entrate~Codice+Codice2)
plot(Entrate, type="l", main="Kodak revenues")
lines(trendlin$fitted, type="o", lty=2)
lines(trendqua$fitted, type="b", lty=4, lwd=2)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Figure 4.6 – Kodak revenues, linear trend and quadratic trend](04-time-series_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-20-1.png){width=480}
:::
:::


