The fluctuations of the seasonally adjusted series now depend only on the trend-cycle which, in this way, can be studied more easily and possibly split into its two elementary components $T$ and $C$.

In other words, the unemployment rate of 11.4% recorded in the first quarter of 1998 is pushed up by the seasonal pattern of the first quarter by 0.3 percentage points; indeed, in the same quarter the seasonally adjusted series records a rate of 11.1%. Likewise, in the third quarter of 2003 the original series stands at 8.0% against 8.3% for the adjusted one, showing this time the favourable effect of the third quarter in reducing the unemployment rate.

Assuming that the trend is linear, since the seasonally adjusted series is $Y_{dest}=T\cdot C$, the cycle can be extracted through the ratio

$$
C=Y_{dest}/T
\tag{4.10}
$$

In our example, the corresponding plot is obtained with the usual commands (Figure 4.9).

