## 4.15 Trigonometric seasonality {#sec-trigonometric}

Another interesting option is to represent the series through a trend-cycle component $f(t)$ and a seasonal component built from sine and cosine functions. For the object *prodotto*, with monthly seasonality, we could estimate the model

$$
Y=\alpha_{0}+\alpha_{1}t+\alpha_{2}t^{2}+\beta_{1}\cos (t2\pi /12)+\beta_{2}\sin (t2\pi /12)
\tag{4.16}
$$

with

::: {.panel-tabset group="language"}
### R


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
tempo <- c(1:72); tempo2 <- tempo^2
coseno <- cos(2*pi/12*tempo)
seno <- sin(2*pi/12*tempo)
modell <- lm(prodotto~tempo+tempo2+coseno+seno)
summary(modell)
